Upper Airway Cough Syndrome (Post-Nasal Drip Complications)
Respiratory System
Sensation of throat dripping, Frequent throat clearing, Cobblestone posterior pharynx, Nighttime cough

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-049
Reason for Visit: Severe, persistent nighttime coughing causing profound sleep deprivation and throat pain, requiring urgent symptomatic relief.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an otherwise well patient experiencing agonizing, tickling coughing fits entirely restricted to nighttime, causing complete sleep disruption and vomiting from gagging. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out asthma or a lower respiratory tract infection prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: As soon as my head hits the pillow, my throat starts tickling uncontrollably, and I cough until I gag, completely ruining my sleep.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been ongoing for 3 weeks following a mild change in weather. The patient describes a chronic, tickling cough that is almost exclusively worse at night or upon awakening. They report a constant sensation of liquid dripping down the back of the throat. The patient complains of frequent, habitual clearing of the throat during the day. They have a known history of allergic rhinitis, experiencing nasal congestion and rhinorrhea. The patient notes mild throat irritation or soreness (from coughing) and hoarseness, but strictly denies any constitutional symptoms (no fever, no weight loss, no severe fatigue beyond sleep deprivation). Halitosis is reported by their partner.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but highly frustrated and fatigued by lack of sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep deprivation impacting daytime work performance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 breaths/min | SpO2: 99% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, visibly tired but not in any respiratory distress. Frequently clearing throat during the interview.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals a classic cobblestone appearance of the posterior pharyngeal mucosa and visible mucoid secretions dripping down the posterior pharynx. Lower respiratory tract auscultation is completely normal, with clear, equal air entry globally and zero wheeze or crackles. The clinical picture is completely isolated to the upper airway.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty cough syrup bottles on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking over-the-counter dry cough suppressants with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Upper Airway Cough Syndrome (Post-Nasal Drip Complications) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Nocturnal Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely clear chest auscultation, absence of wheeze, chest tightness, or dyspnea, and the prominent throat-tickle/dripping sensation which strongly points to an upper airway source.

Laryngopharyngeal Reflux (LPR) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of active allergic rhinitis symptoms (congestion/rhinorrhea) and visible mucoid post-nasal drip, making upper airway secretions the primary culprit, though both can coexist.

Lower Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal chest auscultation, and lack of purulent lower respiratory sputum.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the textbook features of Upper Airway Cough Syndrome (formerly post-nasal drip): a severe nighttime tickling cough, sensation of throat dripping, a cobblestone pharynx, and completely clear lungs. The severe coughing is mechanically triggered by mucus pooling on the vocal cords when the patient lies supine. Deferring clinical assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in another night of agonizing, unmanaged sleep deprivation and unnecessary anxiety regarding a severe chest infection. An urgent after-hours home visit was justified to perform a clear lung exam, provide definitive clinical reassurance, and prescribe targeted empirical therapy (antihistamine/decongestant) to dry up the secretions.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a trial of a first-generation antihistamine (e.g., Promethazine) for nighttime use to aggressively dry secretions and aid sleep, alongside a potent intranasal corticosteroid spray (e.g., Fluticasone) twice daily to address the underlying allergic rhinitis. Advised ceasing useless over-the-counter cough syrups.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform a vigorous saline nasal rinse over the sink exactly 30 minutes before getting into bed; physically washing the accumulated mucus out of your nasal passages directly removes the fluid that would otherwise drip down your throat and trigger the cough reflex when you lie flat.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Saline nasal rinse kit (e.g., neti pot or squeeze bottle).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to assess the efficacy of the nasal steroid spray.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek routine medical care if the cough changes to produce yellow/green phlegm from deep in your chest, if you develop a fever, or if you begin to feel short of breath during the day.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Symptom resolution using these targeted allergy medications confirms the diagnosis; no further complex lung testing is required unless the treatments fail.
Clinical Course: The nighttime antihistamine will provide immediate relief for sleep, but the nasal steroid spray takes 5 to 7 days of continuous use to fully reduce the inflammation.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for an assessment if you start wheezing or feel a tightness in your chest that makes breathing difficult.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your lungs are completely healthy; your nose is overproducing mucus due to allergies, and when you lie flat, this mucus drips directly onto your sensitive vocal cords, triggering a violent reflex to cough it away.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with an extra pillow to prop your head up slightly; utilizing a shallow angle allows gravity to pull the mucus down your esophagus into your stomach, directly preventing it from pooling on your vocal cords and causing the tickle.

